WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:05.220
- We must find a way to
look after one another

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.117
as if we were one single tribe.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.770
- The passing of Chadwick
Boseman has left fans devastated,

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:14.900
not only because of the
impact of his films,

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.570
but because of the lives
he touched as a person.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:19.230
So today we're gonna talk about Chadwick

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:22.330
and his lasting legacy
right now on "The Ketchup."

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:24.460
Since it was announced
that Chadwick Boseman died

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.470
from colon cancer at the age of 43,

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.810
the outpouring of love
and sadness has come

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.140
from all over the world,

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.650
especially from his
peers and collaborators,

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:34.320
like longtime friend Michael B. Jordan,

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.317
who wrote on Instagram,

00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:37.697
"I'm more aware now than
ever that time is short

00:00:37.697 --> 00:00:39.477
with people we love and admire.

00:00:39.477 --> 00:00:41.887
I'm gonna miss your
honesty, your generosity,

00:00:41.887 --> 00:00:44.700
your sense of humor,
and incredible gifts."

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.170
And "Black Panther"
writer-director Ryan Coogler said

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:49.210
in a tribute that, much
like the rest of us,

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.760
he didn't know the details
of Chadwick's illness.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.277
He wrote, "Because he was
a caretaker, a leader,

00:00:54.277 --> 00:00:56.807
and a man of faith, dignity, and pride,

00:00:56.807 --> 00:00:59.277
he shielded his collaborators
from his suffering.

00:00:59.277 --> 00:01:01.597
He lived a beautiful life.
And he made great art.

00:01:01.597 --> 00:01:03.387
Day after day, year after year.

00:01:03.387 --> 00:01:05.010
That was who he was."

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:08.010
And "Shang Chi and the Legend
of the 10 Rings" star Simu Liu

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.767
gave a cool nod when he wrote,

00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:11.327
"I wanna be crystal clear:

00:01:11.327 --> 00:01:13.497
without Chadwick and what
he gave to his character,

00:01:13.497 --> 00:01:15.537
there is no Shang Chi, period.

00:01:15.537 --> 00:01:18.784
My career rides on the
coattails of a great man."

00:01:18.784 --> 00:01:19.862
- [Soldiers] Yibambe!

00:01:19.862 --> 00:01:20.712
- Yibambe!

00:01:20.712 --> 00:01:21.810
- [Soldiers] Yibambe!

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:24.080
- [Naz] And of course it wasn't
just friends and colleagues

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:25.170
who felt this loss.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.350
Countless fans have
shared a similar sentiment

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:28.597
to this Twitter user who said,

00:01:28.597 --> 00:01:31.597
"The way 'Black Panther'
showed so many Black children

00:01:31.597 --> 00:01:33.267
that they could be the superhero,

00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:35.377
his work really meant the world to us.

00:01:35.377 --> 00:01:36.400
Thank you."

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.040
And to sum that up in video
form is this reposting

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:41.870
from the Ron Clark Academy
when a whole school found out

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:44.120
that they were going
to see "Black Panther."

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.390
Chadwick himself
understood and talked about

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:48.340
what the film meant to
the Black community,

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.510
like in this interview
at SiriusXM in 2018.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.020
He broke down while talking about two kids

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:56.210
with terminal cancer that
he was communicating with

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.630
during filming who both eventually passed

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:01.700
and how excited they
were for "Black Panther."

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.803
- Yeah, it means a lot.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.510
- [Naz] But it wasn't just
his role as to T'Challa

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.010
where Chadwick made a difference.

00:02:09.010 --> 00:02:11.440
He played a number of
historical Black figures

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.380
in his short career,

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.720
receiving high praise from
critics and fans each time.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.287
And one fan on Twitter pointed out that

00:02:17.287 --> 00:02:19.597
"His work accelerated the representation

00:02:19.597 --> 00:02:22.127
for African Americans
while also paving the way

00:02:22.127 --> 00:02:26.020
for other POCs, such as Hispanics
and Asians in Hollywood."

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.340
The first of those roles
was as Jackie Robinson

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.280
in 2013's Certified Fresh "42."

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:32.680
The film put Chadwick's name

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.650
and a well-told piece of
history on people's radars.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:36.940
Interestingly and sadly,

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.360
Chadwick passed on this
year's Jackie Robinson Day

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.230
in Major League Baseball that
normally falls on April 15th,

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:44.400
but was rescheduled due to COVID.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.280
And it was 73 years to the
day that Jackie Robinson

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.050
became the first Black man
whites let play baseball.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:52.850
- You will remember me.

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.240
- [Naz] Chadwick also played
the Godfather of Soul,

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.690
James Brown, in 2014's "Get On Up,"

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:59.830
a film that is Certified Fresh at 80%,

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:01.860
mostly thanks to a
performance from Chadwick

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.600
that had critics using
adjectives like "unforgettable,"

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:07.750
"dynamite," and "startling,"
just to name a few.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.623
- When I hit that stage,
your people better be ready,

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:13.700
especially the white folk.

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:15.350
- [Naz] And in 2017's "Marshall,"

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:18.410
Chadwick portrayed Supreme
Court Justice Thurgood Marshall

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:20.250
in the early days of
his career as a lawyer,

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:22.340
once again impressing critics,

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:24.177
like Rolling Stone's
Peter Travers, who said,

00:03:24.177 --> 00:03:26.317
"Charged by Boseman's dramatic lightning,

00:03:26.317 --> 00:03:28.497
'Marshall' gives us an
electrifying glimpse

00:03:28.497 --> 00:03:30.909
of a great man in the making."

00:03:30.909 --> 00:03:32.860
- The only way to get
through a bigot's door

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:34.210
was to break it down.

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:35.970
- [Naz] But beyond his acting prowess

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:37.620
and the important roles that he chose,

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.940
Chadwick was, by all
accounts, a very genuine

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:42.115
and sincere person, both on set

00:03:42.115 --> 00:03:43.731
and when he was out in public.

00:03:43.731 --> 00:03:46.270
Actress Brie Larson echoed
that sentiment on Twitter

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:48.257
by saying, "He took the time to really see

00:03:48.257 --> 00:03:50.807
how you were doing and
gave words of encouragement

00:03:50.807 --> 00:03:52.420
when you felt unsure."

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:54.860
And many on social media
made comments like this one

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:56.217
from Sal on Facebook, who said,

00:03:56.217 --> 00:03:58.147
"He was not only an amazing actor

00:03:58.147 --> 00:04:00.127
but an amazing human being.

00:04:00.127 --> 00:04:02.640
Hollywood lost a great one too soon."

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.490
One strong example of his
wisdom and ability to speak

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:07.550
to others can be found in
this commencement speech

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:11.560
Chadwick gave at his alma mater,
Howard University, in 2018.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:14.403
It is a must-watch and
includes words like this:

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.630
- Purpose is an essential element of you.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.450
It is the reason you are on the planet

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:23.080
at this particular time in history.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:25.660
Your very existence is
wrapped up in the things

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.520
you are here to fulfill.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.410
Whatever you choose for a career path,

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:33.380
remember the struggles
along the way are only meant

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:35.800
to shape you for your purpose.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:39.800
(sweeping instrumental music)

